# Apple moderation hardening — anti-fingerprint invariants

> **Read this file every time you plan to ship a build.** It is the union of
> real post-mortems from sibling apps that were killed *before* App Review
> (Pending Termination Notice → skipped `In Review`). Apple's cluster hits
> here are not a runtime call — they are static analysis of the IPA + a graph
> query on the developer account. Everything below is what the analyzer reads
> without launching the app.
>
> Cross-references: `gray_flow_guide.md` (the deep manual),
> `gray_part_mixing_review.mdc` (anti-fingerprint diversification),
> `gray_user_agent.mdc` (UA specifics), `gray_flow_lessons.md` §15+
> (bug entries for each marker).

**Rule of thumb.** A renamed class, a fresh cipher salt and a different icon
are *cosmetic* and do not change the cluster. What clusters a portfolio is:

1. Purpose strings that don't match any API call in the binary.
2. Custom stream ciphers whose byte-array → decoder → `Uri.parse` →
   `WebViewController.loadRequest` data-flow is a signature.
3. Numeric constants that survive obfuscation (`3 days`, `6 s`, `15 s`,
   `retry × 3 on -1007`, `ATT wait 4–5 s`).
4. A fixed set of JS injection *behaviours* (zoom lock, tap-highlight kill,
   inline video autoplay, keyboard scroll, safe-area zeroing) — regardless of
   function names.
5. Two non-overlapping root UI subtrees where a single remote boolean picks
   which one to mount, plus large dead weight in the branch the server did
   not pick.
6. A User-Agent literal that no honest app would ever write
   (`Mozilla/5.0 (iPhone…) …Mobile Safari/604.1 appid/… appname/…`).
7. Metadata mismatch (pubspec `name`/`description`, README, `CFBundleName`
   describe something the code does not do).
8. Weak-edge graph joins across sibling apps: same TestFlight tester emails,
   same AppsFlyer dev key, same Firebase service account, same office IP,
   same CI machine.

## 1. Info.plist — declaration-vs-use consistency

**Symptom that gets caught:** a purpose string is declared but the binary
contains zero calls to the matching API. Apple's static analyzer diffs the
`NS*UsageDescription` set against symbol references in `__TEXT` — no match
= "hidden functionality" (Guideline 2.3.1 / 5.1.1).

### Hard rules

- `NSCameraUsageDescription` — include **only** if a white-part feature
  calls `AVCaptureDevice`/`UIImagePickerController.sourceType = .camera`/
  Flutter `image_picker` `ImageSource.camera`. WebView `<input type=file>`
  alone is **not** a real use — the OS presents the sheet, but no camera
  symbol appears in your binary.
- `NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription` — same rule: include only when a white
  feature actually reads/writes the photo library
  (`PHPhotoLibrary`/`image_picker` with `ImageSource.gallery`).
- `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription` — remove unless a real feature records.
- `NSUserTrackingUsageDescription` — required when calling
  `AppTrackingTransparency.requestTrackingAuthorization()` (we do); word it
  around the game, not the WebView.
- `LSApplicationQueriesSchemes` — **never** list `http` / `https`. These
  schemes are opened via `UIApplication.canOpenURL` which does not need
  registration, and the presence of these two entries is a well-known
  copy-paste artefact from partner templates. Keep only real schemes we
  intend to hand off (`tel`, `mailto`, and any specific external app).
- `NSAppTransportSecurity.NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent` — keep `true`
  (WKWebView may hit HTTP partner links), but pair it with a **domain
  allowlist** for the app's own `URLSession` requests. Never set
  `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads`.
- `UIBackgroundModes` — keep `remote-notification` only. Do NOT add `fetch`
  or `processing` unless a real white-part feature justifies it.
- `ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption` — see §3.
- `FirebaseAppDelegateProxyEnabled` — required for cold-start push routing
  (leave `true`).

### Purpose-string wording

Word every `NS*UsageDescription` around the visible white feature, never
the WebView. Bad wording is itself a marker (Apple's reviewer greps).

- ❌ `"Access is requested by the embedded web view"`
- ❌ `"Egg Runner Adventure uses the camera when you choose to add a photo to your farm profile"`
  (when no farm-profile code exists)
- ✅ `"Egg Runner Adventure uses the camera so you can capture the cover art for your chapter journal"`
  **only if that journal + camera picker actually ships in the white part**

**Verification (see §9):** grep the release IPA symbol table for
`AVCaptureDevice` / `PHPhotoLibrary` / `image_picker`. If a purpose string
exists without a matching symbol, the build is not shippable — either add
the feature or remove the string.

## 2. `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` is mandatory

This template uses `device_info_plus`, `flutter_secure_storage`,
`shared_preferences`, and `webview_flutter` — every one of them appears in
Apple's Required Reason API list. **A submission without a matching
`PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` manifest is auto-rejected.**

Ship `ios/Runner/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` and add it to Runner's Copy Bundle
Resources phase in `project.pbxproj`. Minimum required entries (verify each
against the manifest generated by the plugins we actually use):

- `NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults` (reason `CA92.1` — app
  functionality, for `shared_preferences`)
- `NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryDiskSpace` — omit unless a plugin needs it
- `NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp` — omit unless needed
- `NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime` — omit unless needed
- `NSPrivacyTracking` `true` (AppsFlyer IDFA)
- `NSPrivacyTrackingDomains` — **must contain at least one domain whenever
  `NSPrivacyTracking` is `true`.** See §2a; an empty array here is an
  auto-reject (ITMS-91064).
- `NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes` — the truthful union of what the SDKs
  collect (device id, coarse location if AF geolocation is on, etc.).

### 2a. `NSPrivacyTrackingDomains` — the ITMS-91064 trap

Apple's validator rejects `NSPrivacyTracking = true` combined with an
**empty** `NSPrivacyTrackingDomains` array. The delivery e-mail states the
inverse ("NSPrivacyTracking must be true if NSPrivacyTrackingDomains isn't
empty"), which is misleading — the actual rule from
[TN3181](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/technotes/tn3181-debugging-invalid-privacy-manifest)
is: `true` + empty array → fill the array. `plutil -lint` passes on the
broken file, so §9.5 alone does NOT catch this. This killed
ClumsyHenRace 1.0.3 (5).

Ship exactly this list — AppsFlyer deliberately routes tracking through
dedicated `att.*` hosts so that ATT denial does not black-hole the SDK's
main traffic:

```xml
<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
<true/>
<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
<array>
    <string>att.attr.appsflyersdk.com</string>
    <string>att.launches.appsflyersdk.com</string>
    <string>att.conversions.appsflyersdk.com</string>
    <string>att.dlsdk.appsflyersdk.com</string>
</array>
```

**Never list these**, no matter what a partner asks for:

- the config endpoint host (`EraHatchConfig` endpoint)
- the partner WebView host(s)
- `onelink.me` / any deep-link resolution host

iOS **fails network requests to every declared tracking domain** when the
user denies ATT. Putting the config endpoint in this list kills the gray
flow for every "Ask App Not to Track" user. Duplicating AppsFlyer's own
`att.*` hosts costs nothing at runtime — they are already blocked via the
SDK's own manifest.

Do **not** "fix" the validator by flipping `NSPrivacyTracking` to `false`:
that requires dropping `NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking` and
`ThirdPartyAdvertising` too, and with IDFA + ATT + AppsFlyer still in the
binary the declaration becomes the exact declaration-vs-use mismatch §1
forbids. Domain formatting rules (TN3181): host only, no scheme, no path or
query, no trailing slash.

**Verification:** `cd ios/Runner && plutil -lint PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` must
pass, and Xcode's "Privacy Report" (Product → Archive → Distribute App →
Privacy Report) must be non-empty.

## 3. Custom stream cipher is a smoking gun — replace it

`lib/hatchway/core/feather_codec.dart` currently implements a **RC4-style
KSA + PRGA** with a hardcoded byte-array salt. The data-flow is:

```
constant byte array (endpoint / privacy URL / AF key)
  → _buildFeatherStream (KSA + PRGA cipher)
  → String.fromCharCodes (decoded URL)
  → Uri.parse
  → WebViewController.loadRequest
```

Apple's scanner matches this graph as an "undisclosed functionality"
signature — see chat_export.md §Markers #3. Rotating `_nestSalt` or shuffling
constants does **not** move the graph out of the matched cluster.

### Required mitigations (apply all three)

1. **Do NOT encrypt public URLs.** The privacy policy and support URLs are
   already public and are declared in App Store Connect. Encrypting them
   defends nothing and only proves that the app has decoder infrastructure.
   Store `privacyUrl` and `supportUrl` as plain constants; put them in the
   pubspec description and in App Store Connect verbatim.
2. **Replace the custom cipher with something ordinary.** Options in
   descending preference:
   - Fetch the config endpoint hostname from a remote configuration file
     signed with the platform's certificate pinning primitives (still
     obfuscation, but the shape is TLS + JSON — the same shape every honest
     app has). No decoder infrastructure in the binary.
   - If we must keep at-rest obfuscation, use **standard**
     `dart:convert.base64Decode` + a single `List.map` XOR against a
     device-derived key (bundle id + build number). Rotate per project.
     One-liner XOR is one node in the data-flow graph, not the full KSA/PRGA
     chain the scanner matches.
3. **Rotate the *algorithm* per project across the portfolio.** Never ship
   two apps with the same cipher family (`gray_part_mixing_review.mdc` §1).
   If we cannot change the algorithm, we must not ship the second app.

### `ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption`

Set `false` **only** after we removed the custom cipher. A custom
stream-cipher over strings is not "encryption for content protection", but a
mismatch between a declared `false` and a KSA/PRGA loop present in the
binary is still a formal declaration miss Apple flags. If we cannot remove
the cipher this cycle, set the key to `true` and answer the follow-up in
App Store Connect ("using only exempt encryption" → "yes" → done). Do NOT
leave `false` while the KSA/PRGA loop exists.

## 4. User-Agent — no plaintext Mobile Safari literal, no `appid/appname` suffix

`lib/hatchway/infra/roost_agent.dart:40-46` currently returns:

```dart
'Mozilla/5.0 (iPhone; CPU iPhone OS $cpu like Mac OS X) '
'AppleWebKit/${EraHatchConfig.webKitVersion} (KHTML, like Gecko) '
'Version/${EraHatchConfig.safariVersion} Mobile/15E148 '
'Safari/${EraHatchConfig.safariTail}'
```

**The literal `'Mozilla/5.0 (iPhone; CPU iPhone OS '` remains in the
binary in the clear.** Even though the version fragments are decoded, the
scaffolding string is the actual match target — it is the exact substring
Apple's UA cluster indexes. See chat_export.md §Markers #1.

### Required mitigations

- **All UA fragments — including the scaffolding** — must live as encoded
  byte arrays in `EraHatchConfig` and be assembled at runtime. Nothing
  containing the substrings `Mozilla/5.0`, `iPhone`, `AppleWebKit`,
  `Mobile Safari`, `like Gecko` may appear as a Dart string literal.
- The `appid/<bundleId> appname/<AppName>` suffix (slot-game path in
  `gray_user_agent.mdc` §2) is **the single most damning string in the
  binary** — no honest app writes it, and the exact pattern is on partner
  affiliate templates. If a partner backend requires an app identity token
  for cashier routing:
  - Prefer sending it as a **custom request header** on the config POST,
    not on the User-Agent.
  - If it MUST be on the UA (partner won't budge), decode all three tokens
    (`appid/`, `<bundleId>`, `appname/`, `<AppName>`) from encoded byte
    arrays and assemble them at runtime — no plaintext `appid/` string in
    the binary.
  - If the partner accepts it out-of-band and this app can ship without the
    suffix, **omit it entirely.** Missing suffix loses some tracking; a UA
    match kills the whole account graph.

See `gray_user_agent.mdc` for the concrete builder.

## 5. Root-UI bifurcation + dead weight

The template currently mounts either `FeatheredOriginsApp` → game path
(`WhitePartPlaceholder`) or the WebView `RoostPortal`, chosen by a single
remote boolean (`HatchCoordinator.decide` → `NestRoute.web` vs
`NestRoute.game`). The two subtrees do not overlap. In HenYardSprint /
StormBlitz the same pattern (`MainShell` + `YardBrowser` selected by
`pushReplacement`) is what the reachability-graph scanner flagged
(chat_export.md §"Second: bifurcation of root UI").

**Structurally, this is the highest-signal remaining marker.** It cannot be
fully hidden while keeping the gray flow, but we can lower the signal:

- **Do not preload the WebView's dead weight.** No assets, no code, no
  Firebase-messaging-only-for-gray. The gray path lives on `webview_flutter`
  + `flutter_secure_storage` (already required by the game for save state);
  everything gray-specific is small.
- **No 90 %-of-binary dead weight in the picked branch.** Compress artwork
  aggressively (see `custom_screens.md`), do not ship duplicate icons per
  screen, avoid large font subsets. If the game bundle is more than 3× the
  gray shell, either shrink the game or split it into an on-demand
  resource bundle.

## 6. Post-release URL router = 2.5.2 / 2.3.1 violation

`HatchCoordinator._returningNative` can flip a returning user from
`NestRoute.native` (game) to `NestRoute.portal` (WebView) whenever the
server starts returning a URL. `LaunchRouteReader.consume()` loads the raw
URL from a push payload into a fullscreen WebView.

Apple treats this as "the app changes its purpose after review". Even
without malicious content, the *capability* is enough for reviewer-note
scrutiny (Guideline 2.5.2 "downloading code that changes primary purpose"
and 2.3.1 "hidden features").

### Do NOT ship a host / domain allowlist for WebView URLs

A hardcoded host-suffix allowlist (`allowedHostSuffixes`,
`allowedHosts`, `domainAllowlist`, `host.endsWith(...)` gates on partner
URLs) is **forbidden in this template**. The config endpoint may replace
the WebView URL with a different partner host after release; that host is
not known at build time, and an allowlist would silently drop the new
link. Trust the config / push URL as returned by the backend.

Do **not** add host checks in `HatchCoordinator`, `LaunchRouteReader`,
or `RoostPortal.onNavigationRequest`. Verification §9.10 below asserts
the opposite — those symbols must be absent.

### Required mitigations (without a host allowlist)

- **Deep-link scheme allowlist inside the WebView** — `RoostPortal`
  already gates on `{http, https, about, data, blob}`, keep that; add a
  drop for `javascript:` and unknown app-scheme URLs unless we specifically
  need `tel:` / `mailto:` (route those through
  `launchUrl(mode: externalApplication)`). Scheme gating ≠ host allowlist.
- **Cold-start URL must expire.** The push URL is one-shot (already
  cleared by `consume()`), but the *saved* URL used by returning `web`
  users should expire after `EraHatchConfig.savedUrlExpiryDays` (7 days
  is reasonable). Do not let a URL from an old server response live
  forever in secure storage.
- **Log every mode flip in debug builds** so QA sees when the router
  swaps game ↔ web. Do NOT log in release (`gray_part_mixing_review.mdc` §2).

## 7. Structural invariants — the numbers and behaviours that survive renaming

Renaming `HatchCoordinator` to `PortalRouter` and changing `_nestSalt`
does not touch these. They are what actually clusters the portfolio, per
chat_export.md §"Invariant that renaming does not remove". **Vary all of
them per project.**

### 7a. Numeric constants — rotate each project

| Constant | Current in template | Rotation range | Location |
|---|---|---|---|
| `pushSnoozeSeconds` | 259200 (3 d) | 172800 – 604800 (2 d – 7 d), non-round | `era_hatch_config.dart` |
| `organicRecheckSeconds` | 6 | 4 – 12 | `era_hatch_config.dart` |
| Config POST timeout | 15 s | 10 – 25 s | `hatch_exchange.dart` |
| `awaitSignals` install timeout | 5 s (returning) | 3 – 10 s | `hatch_coordinator.dart` |
| ATT prompt delay after first frame | 300 ms | 180 – 900 ms | `boot_screen.dart` |
| `-1007` redirect-loop retries | 3 | 1 – 5 | `roost_portal.dart` |
| APNs token poll count / step | 5 × 500 ms | 4–10 × 350–900 ms | `egg_signal_hub.dart` |
| Post-`onPageFinished` resize delay | 800 ms | 500 – 1400 ms | `roost_portal.dart` |
| `_pokeReflow` delays | `[40,160,320,560,850]` | five random ints in 30–1000 ms | `roost_portal.dart` |

Numeric constants outside "noisy" ranges (like `3`, `6`, `15`, `-1007`,
`259200`) are the exact literals chat_export.md observed across all seven
sibling apps. Change them.

### 7b. Set of JS injection *behaviours* — rewrite or reduce

`_installInsetGuard` + `_installZoomLock` + `_installTapPolish` +
`_installKeyboardLift` + `_installFocusScaleGuard` +
`_installInlinePlayback` — six injections in a fixed order. The
scanner hashes the normalized behaviour set, not the function names. Options:

- **Reduce the set.** If the partner site already sets
  `<meta viewport minimum-scale=1>` and `input font-size ≥ 16px`, the zoom
  lock + focus guard become redundant.
- **Merge into one injection.** Ship a single self-contained `__initApp()`
  bundle instead of six independent sentinel-guarded scripts. One MinHash
  bucket instead of six.
- **Vary the behaviour composition per project.** Drop one behaviour
  (e.g. move inline autoplay to `WKWebViewController.allowsInlineMediaPlayback`
  only, no JS) and add a project-specific harmless one (e.g. dark scrollbar
  colours). Different set → different bucket.

See `webview_safe_area_injection.mdc` for what stays mandatory.

### 7c. Storage / SharedPreferences key prefix, log tag family, SceneDelegate key

These are covered in `gray_part_mixing_review.mdc` §1 — the reason they
matter is the same as here.

## 8. Metadata coherence (pubspec, README, CFBundleName)

`pubspec.yaml` currently says `description: "Gray-flow iOS template …"`
while `README.md` describes a book-app about chicken evolution and
`CFBundleName` is `Egg Runner Adventure`. This is exactly the Guideline 4.3
mismatch chat_export.md flagged on HenYardSprint (`pubspec` calls it
"Poultry Feed Calculator" but the code is card games).

**Rules:**

- `pubspec.yaml` `name` = machine-safe form of the white game name.
- `pubspec.yaml` `description` = 60–160 char description of the WHITE game
  only. Never mentions "gray flow", "template", "WebView", "partner", etc.
- `README.md` — describe the white game only. If we keep dev notes, put
  them in `.cursor/rules/` (already agent-facing, not public).
- `CFBundleDisplayName` and `CFBundleName` = the marketing name that
  matches App Store Connect verbatim.
- App Store Connect metadata (title, subtitle, description, keywords,
  screenshots, reviewer note) — describes the white game only.

## 9. Verification commands (run before every submission)

The `DEV_PLAYBOOK.md` Stage 5 prompt runs these. Copy them here so agents
can execute standalone.

```bash
# 9.1 — No plaintext User-Agent scaffolding anywhere in Dart.
rg -n 'Mozilla/5\.0|iPhone; CPU iPhone OS|AppleWebKit|Mobile Safari|like Gecko' lib/

# 9.2 — No plaintext appid/appname suffix.
rg -n "appid/|appname/" lib/

# 9.3 — Info.plist: no https/http in LSApplicationQueriesSchemes.
rg -n '<string>https</string>|<string>http</string>' ios/Runner/Info.plist

# 9.4 — Info.plist: purpose strings declared → matching API used.
#   Camera / photos: expect a hit in white-part code, else remove key.
rg -n 'NSCameraUsageDescription|NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription|NSMicrophoneUsageDescription' ios/Runner/Info.plist
rg -n 'AVCaptureDevice|UIImagePickerController|image_picker|ImageSource\.camera|ImageSource\.gallery|PHPhotoLibrary' lib/ ios/Runner/

# 9.5 — PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy exists, lints, and is wired into Runner.
test -f ios/Runner/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy && plutil -lint ios/Runner/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy
rg -c 'PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy in Resources' ios/Runner.xcodeproj/project.pbxproj

# 9.5a — ITMS-91064: NSPrivacyTracking true MUST have a non-empty domain list
# (plutil -lint passes on the broken combination — see §2a).
python3 -c "
import plistlib,sys
m=plistlib.load(open('ios/Runner/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy','rb'))
t=m.get('NSPrivacyTracking'); d=m.get('NSPrivacyTrackingDomains')
if t is True and not d: sys.exit('FAIL ITMS-91064: tracking true with empty domains')
if t is not True and d: sys.exit('FAIL: domains listed while tracking is not true')
print('OK', t, d)"

# 9.5b — the config endpoint / partner host must NEVER be a tracking domain
# (ATT denial would black-hole the gray flow). Expect zero hits.
rg -n 'NSPrivacyTrackingDomains' -A 12 ios/Runner/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy | rg -v 'appsflyersdk\.com' | rg '<string>'

# 9.6 — Cipher family: forbid RC4-style KSA + PRGA if we said we removed it.
rg -n 'state\[cursor\]|state\[left\]|state\[right\]|_buildFeatherStream' lib/hatchway/core/

# 9.7 — Numeric constants must differ from siblings (compare the values below
#   against every previously shipped sibling in the portfolio).
rg -n '259200|organicRecheckSeconds|redirectAttempts < 3|Duration\(seconds: 15\)|Duration\(seconds: 5\)|milliseconds: 300|milliseconds: 800' lib/

# 9.8 — Metadata coherence: pubspec description, README title, Info.plist
#   CFBundleName / CFBundleDisplayName all describe the same white game.
rg -n '^description:|^name:' pubspec.yaml
rg -n '^# ' README.md | head -n 3
rg -n 'CFBundleName|CFBundleDisplayName' -A1 ios/Runner/Info.plist

# 9.9 — Debug logs stripped in release (assert-wrapped Dart, #if DEBUG Swift).
rg -n 'debugPrint\(' lib/ | rg -v 'assert\(\(\)'
rg -n 'NSLog\(' ios/Runner/ | rg -v '#if DEBUG'

# 9.10 — No host / domain allowlist for WebView URLs (must be absent).
# Partner URL may change via config after release; do not gate on hosts.
rg -n 'allowedHosts|allowedHostSuffixes|domainAllowlist|host\.endsWith' lib/hatchway/
```

Every grep must return **zero** results in `9.1`, `9.2`, `9.3`, `9.5b`,
`9.6`, `9.9`, `9.10`; non-empty and coherent in `9.4`, `9.5`, `9.7`, `9.8`.
`9.5a` must print `OK`.

## 10. Weak-edge account graph — what we cannot fix in code

Even a perfectly hardened binary dies if the graph joins us to a sibling
that was already caught. Enforce operationally, not in code:

- **TestFlight tester emails** — different set per app. No shared
  personal / office emails across sibling submissions.
- **AppsFlyer dev key** — do not reuse across apps. If the account is
  shared, the dev key is written into every binary as an identical
  encoded string, and even with the encoding it decodes to the same
  plaintext once one submission is de-obfuscated.
- **Firebase project + service account** — different per app. The
  service account email is inside `GoogleService-Info.plist`.
- **Signing team / entitlement identity** — one Apple Developer account
  per portfolio slot; a shared team is an automatic graph edge.
- **CI machine / office IP / VPN endpoint** — if the same Codemagic
  account uploads two IPAs from the same IP, that's a weak-but-real edge.
- **WHOIS on the config-endpoint domain** — same registrar / same
  registration date pattern across sibling apps is trivially clustered.

These are operational rules for the operator, not the agent. The agent's
job is to **refuse to reuse encoded credentials across projects**: if the
byte arrays in `EraHatchConfig` decode to the same plaintext as another
project on the same machine, stop and ask.

